    <item>
      <title>Ultrasound-Guided Suprascapular Nerve Block Versus Intravenous Fentanyl for Analgesia in Patients Presenting to the Emergency Department With Traumatic Scapular Fractures</title>
      <link>https://proxy1.library.jhu.edu/login?qurl=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.1016%2Fj.annemergmed.2026.09.009</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">pmid:43043325</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 10:29:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <category>Annals of emergency medicine</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Study objective:&lt;/b&gt; To compare the analgesic efficacy and safety of ultrasound-guided suprascapular nerve block with intravenous fentanyl in patients with traumatic scapular fractures.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Methods:&lt;/b&gt; We conducted an open-label randomized controlled pilot trial in the emergency department (ED) of a Level I trauma center in North India. Adults with traumatic scapular fractures and Defense and Veterans Pain Rating Scale scores of 7 and higher, were randomly assigned to ultrasound-guided suprascapular nerve block or intravenous fentanyl (1 μg/kg). Pain scores were assessed at baseline and 30, 60, 120, 240, and 360 minutes. Secondary outcomes included rescue analgesia and adverse events.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Results:&lt;/b&gt; Sixty patients were randomly assigned to the 2 groups. Median baseline pain score was 10 in both groups. Pain scores were consistently lower with suprascapular nerve block, with median scores of 3 (interquartile [IQR] 2 to 5) versus 7 (IQR 6 to 8) at 30 minutes. Between-group differences were 4 (95% confidence interval [CI], 3 to 4) at 30 and 60 minutes, 2 (95% CI, 2 to 2) at 120 and 240 minutes, and 1 (95% CI, 1 to 2) at 360 minutes. Rescue analgesia was less frequent with suprascapular nerve block (13% versus 79%; risk difference, -66% [95% CI, 47.5 to 85.2]). Drowsiness was more common with fentanyl (12 versus 2 participants); hypotension occurred in 1 fentanyl recipient and paresthesia in 1 suprascapular nerve block recipient.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Conclusion:&lt;/b&gt; In this pilot randomized trial, ultrasound-guided suprascapular nerve block provided greater and sustained pain relief, reduced rescue analgesia, and fewer systemic adverse effects than intravenous fentanyl in adults with traumatic scapular fractures. Larger multicenter trials are needed to confirm these findings and evaluate cumulative opioid use and longer-term outcomes.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Chanda A, Bhoi S, Sinha TP, Gopinath B, Sahu AK, Gangdev A&lt;br/&gt;Journal Article&lt;br/&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://proxy1.library.jhu.edu/login?qurl=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.1016%2Fj.annemergmed.2026.09.009&quot;&gt;Full text&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a href=&quot;https://proxy1.library.jhu.edu/login?qurl=https%3A%2F%2Fpubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov%2F43043325%2F&quot;&gt;PubMed&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
